.TH VM_MAP 9 "January 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Kernel Developer's Manual"
.SH NAME
vm_map \- virtual address space management
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/vm/vm_map.h>
.LP
.BI "void vm_map_init(vm_map_t *" map ", pmap_t " pmap ", uintptr_t " min ", uintptr_t " max ");"
.br
.BI "vm_map_t *vm_map_create(pmap_t " pmap ", uintptr_t " min ", uintptr_t " max ");"
.br
.BI "void vm_map_destroy(vm_map_t *" map ");"
.br
.BI "int vm_map_insert(vm_map_t *" map ", struct vm_object *" obj ", uint64_t " offset ", uintptr_t " start ", uintptr_t " end ");"
.br
.BI "int vm_map_remove(vm_map_t *" map ", uintptr_t " start ", uintptr_t " end ");"
.br
.BI "int vm_map_find_space(vm_map_t *" map ", uintptr_t *" addr ", size_t " length ");"
.br
.BI "vm_map_entry_t *vm_map_lookup(vm_map_t *" map ", uintptr_t " va ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B vm_map
module manages virtual address spaces. Each process has a
.B vm_map_t
that describes its valid memory regions via a linked list of
.B vm_map_entry_t
structures.
.SS Structures
.B vm_map_t
contains:
.IP \[bu] 2
.B pmap
\- machine-dependent page tables
.IP \[bu]
.B header
\- sentinel node for entry list
.IP \[bu]
.B nentries
\- count of entries
.IP \[bu]
.B min_offset/max_offset
\- address space bounds
.PP
.B vm_map_entry_t
describes a contiguous region:
.IP \[bu] 2
.B start/end
\- virtual address range
.IP \[bu]
.B object
\- backing vm_object
.IP \[bu]
.B offset
\- offset into object
.IP \[bu]
.B protection
\- access permissions
.SS Functions
.B vm_map_create
allocates and initializes a new map.
.PP
.B vm_map_destroy
frees all entries and the map itself.
.PP
.B vm_map_insert
adds a new mapping backed by
.IR obj .
.PP
.B vm_map_remove
unmaps a range, splitting or trimming entries as needed.
.PP
.B vm_map_find_space
locates a free region of the requested size.
.PP
.B vm_map_lookup
returns the entry containing
.IR va ,
or NULL if not mapped.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.B vm_map_create
returns NULL on allocation failure.
.PP
.B vm_map_insert, vm_map_remove, vm_map_find_space
return 0 on success, -1 on error.
.PP
.B vm_map_lookup
returns the entry or NULL.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vm_object (9),
.BR vm_fault (9),
.BR pmap (9)
